I can’t provide a legal opinion that the report is privileged, especially without seeing it. “Confidential” or “quality improvement” labels alone do not create legal professional privilege.

Privilege commonly depends on:

- the jurisdiction and applicable legislation;
- who commissioned the report and for what dominant purpose;
- whether litigation was existing or reasonably anticipated;
- whether confidential communications involved a lawyer giving legal advice;
- who prepared, received, or later circulated the report; and
- whether privilege was waived through disclosure or mixed operational purposes.

Have your organisation’s lawyer review the report, commissioning instructions, distribution history, and governing incident-review policy before asserting privilege or releasing it. Preserve the original and access records, restrict further circulation, and do not alter or retrospectively relabel it.

If you share a de-identified copy and state the jurisdiction—such as NSW, another Australian state, or New Zealand—I can identify the relevant factual indicators and questions for counsel, but the final privilege determination must remain with an authorised lawyer.
